{"id":"arms-of-government","title":"Government: the three arms of government","subject":"Government","label":"JAMB · WAEC","exams":["jamb","waec"],"language":"en","seconds":179,"duration":179.3,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":471,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"Three arms","start":0,"end":11.625},{"id":"explain","title":"Core idea","start":11.625,"end":50.785},{"id":"explain2","title":"More functions","start":50.785,"end":96.655},{"id":"example","title":"Worked example","start":96.655,"end":112.325},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":112.325,"end":119.055},{"id":"answer","title":"Answer","start":119.055,"end":126.385},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common slips","start":126.385,"end":148.395},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap","start":148.395,"end":179.3}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":6.31,"text":"Have you ever wondered who actually makes the laws in Nigeria, who carries them out, and who settles disputes?","w":[0.52,0.68,0.86,1.08,1.42,1.66,2.02,2.28,2.42,2.72,2.84,3.78,3.96,4.3,4.52,5.04,5.14,5.32,5.68]},{"s":0,"a":6.76,"b":11.2,"text":"I'm Kora. Today we'll look at the three arms of government and how they work together.","w":[6.88,7.04,7.88,8.26,8.46,8.66,8.76,8.88,9.24,9.48,9.6,10.1,10.24,10.42,10.58,10.78]},{"s":1,"a":12.05,"b":16.8,"text":"Government has three arms, and each has a clear job. The legislature makes the laws.","w":[12.1,12.54,12.78,13.1,13.6,13.84,14.06,14.24,14.32,14.6,15.32,15.44,16.06,16.3,16.42]},{"s":1,"a":17.1,"b":26.34,"text":"In Nigeria, under the nineteen ninety nine Constitution, that's the National Assembly. It has two parts: the Senate and the House of Representatives.","w":[17.18,17.32,18.38,18.62,18.8,19.3,19.6,19.94,21.14,21.4,21.56,22,22.84,22.94,23.18,23.4,24.18,24.38,24.94,25.06,25.2,25.48,25.58]},{"s":1,"a":26.64,"b":29.71,"text":"At state level, these are the State Houses of Assembly.","w":[26.7,26.86,27.18,27.9,28.16,28.3,28.44,28.8,29.18,29.28]},{"s":1,"a":30.01,"b":37.69,"text":"The executive carries out the laws and runs the day to day government. In Nigeria, that's the President, the Vice President and ministers.","w":[30.02,30.2,30.84,31.22,31.36,31.5,31.82,31.98,32.28,32.4,32.58,32.68,32.88,33.98,34.08,34.78,35.06,35.16,36,36.14,36.42,36.92,37.06]},{"s":1,"a":37.99,"b":42.06,"text":"In the states, it's the Governor and commissioners, working with the civil service.","w":[38.06,38.16,38.3,38.82,39,39.14,39.58,39.7,40.64,40.98,41.12,41.24,41.5]},{"s":1,"a":42.36,"b":50.36,"text":"The judiciary interprets the laws and settles disputes. In Nigeria, that's the courts, headed by the Supreme Court and the Chief Justice of Nigeria.","w":[42.38,42.52,43.28,43.8,43.92,44.28,44.44,44.78,45.72,45.84,46.54,46.78,46.92,47.52,47.82,47.98,48.1,48.5,48.82,48.92,49.04,49.32,49.68,49.8]},{"s":2,"a":51.21,"b":57.56,"text":"Each arm has extra duties. The legislature approves the budget, confirms appointments and oversees the executive.","w":[51.26,51.56,51.76,52.04,52.32,53.12,53.26,53.94,54.32,54.42,55.12,55.6,56.18,56.4,56.92,57.04]},{"s":2,"a":57.86,"b":63.36,"text":"The executive prepares the budget, signs bills, conducts foreign relations and maintains law and order.","w":[57.88,58.04,58.56,58.98,59.1,59.78,60.18,60.98,61.38,61.68,62.22,62.32,62.78,62.96,63.1]},{"s":2,"a":63.66,"b":70.94,"text":"The judiciary protects citizens' rights and can declare an action unconstitutional. That power is called judicial review.","w":[63.68,63.82,64.58,65.02,65.56,66.24,66.38,66.54,67.02,67.24,67.6,69.02,69.26,69.68,69.8,70.08,70.56]},{"s":2,"a":71.24,"b":78.2,"text":"Separation of powers, linked to Baron de Montesquieu, means the three functions are in different hands so no one becomes too powerful.","w":[71.34,71.9,72.02,72.52,72.78,72.94,73.28,73.4,74.44,74.7,74.84,75.14,75.66,75.78,75.92,76.24,76.6,76.84,77.08,77.22,77.56,77.74]},{"s":2,"a":78.5,"b":84.24,"text":"Checks and balances means each arm limits the others. For example, the President can veto a bill.","w":[78.6,78.96,79.12,79.72,80.08,80.4,80.64,81,81.16,81.84,82.02,82.78,82.94,83.38,83.54,83.9,83.96]},{"s":2,"a":84.54,"b":90.48,"text":"The legislature can override the veto with a two thirds majority and can impeach. The courts can strike down a law.","w":[84.58,84.72,85.34,85.58,86,86.1,86.44,86.6,86.68,86.92,87.24,87.76,87.88,88.04,88.84,89.02,89.34,89.52,89.82,90.1,90.16]},{"s":2,"a":90.78,"b":96.23,"text":"Ghana and other West African countries have the same three arms, with a Parliament as the legislature.","w":[90.82,91.24,91.48,91.7,92.04,92.4,92.86,93.06,93.24,93.58,93.98,94.6,94.78,94.86,95.44,95.54,95.66]},{"s":3,"a":97.08,"b":101.97,"text":"Imagine a new law on school safety. The legislature debates it and passes the bill.","w":[97.12,97.54,97.62,97.86,98.26,98.4,98.72,99.62,99.76,100.38,100.82,100.96,101.1,101.5,101.6]},{"s":3,"a":102.27,"b":106.76,"text":"The executive, through the President, signs it and the ministry carries it out.","w":[102.3,102.48,103.36,103.6,103.72,104.62,105.02,105.34,105.44,105.56,106,106.34,106.5]},{"s":3,"a":107.06,"b":111.9,"text":"If someone challenges it, the judiciary interprets it and decides if it's constitutional.","w":[107.14,107.3,107.7,108.24,108.48,108.62,109.34,109.9,110.24,110.36,110.84,110.96,111.12]},{"s":4,"a":112.75,"b":115.93,"text":"Quick check: which arm of government interprets the law?","w":[112.8,113.06,113.9,114.26,114.46,114.58,115.02,115.54,115.66]},{"s":4,"a":116.38,"b":118.63,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[116.44,116.74,117.04,117.18,117.48,118.08,118.26]},{"s":5,"a":119.48,"b":125.96,"text":"The judiciary interprets the law. The executive enforces it, while the courts settle disputes and apply the law.","w":[119.5,119.62,120.48,121,121.12,121.82,121.98,122.6,123.16,123.68,123.84,123.94,124.26,124.56,125.12,125.24,125.56,125.66]},{"s":6,"a":126.81,"b":133.13,"text":"Avoid three common mistakes. First, don't say the executive makes laws; the legislature makes them.","w":[126.86,127.3,127.62,127.98,129.14,129.66,129.96,130.2,130.34,130.9,131.18,131.98,132.14,132.7,132.92]},{"s":6,"a":133.43,"b":141.2,"text":"Second, separation of powers means the functions are in different hands, while checks and balances means each arm limits the others.","w":[133.58,134.44,135.06,135.18,135.68,135.94,136.14,136.66,136.8,136.96,137.28,137.96,138.22,138.56,138.68,139.26,139.64,139.98,140.24,140.58,140.74]},{"s":6,"a":141.5,"b":147.97,"text":"Third, don't say the judiciary enforces laws; the executive enforces, the judiciary interprets.","w":[141.52,142.28,142.56,142.78,142.9,143.6,144.16,145.04,145.2,145.76,146.56,146.7,147.34]},{"s":7,"a":148.82,"b":155.6,"text":"Recap: the legislature makes laws, the executive implements them, and the judiciary interprets them.","w":[148.92,149.7,149.88,150.6,150.94,151.72,151.9,152.58,153.06,153.7,153.84,153.96,154.8,155.38]},{"s":7,"a":155.9,"b":163.57,"text":"In Nigeria, the National Assembly is bicameral, the executive includes the President and ministers, and the judiciary is headed by the Supreme Court.","w":[155.96,156.08,156.76,156.9,157.3,157.78,157.9,158.8,158.92,159.46,159.82,159.92,160.44,160.54,161.42,161.54,161.64,162.32,162.44,162.68,162.82,162.94,163.32]},{"s":7,"a":163.87,"b":170.02,"text":"Separation of powers keeps each arm in its own lane, and checks and balances let each arm limit the others.","w":[163.98,164.56,164.68,165.1,165.42,165.78,165.96,166.06,166.28,166.42,167.14,167.32,167.64,167.78,168.36,168.62,168.92,169.16,169.44,169.58]},{"s":7,"a":170.32,"b":175.53,"text":"Exam tip: in JAMB and WAEC questions, match each function to the correct arm.","w":[170.42,170.88,171.5,171.66,172.08,172.24,172.68,173.62,174.06,174.3,174.7,174.82,174.92,175.3]},{"s":7,"a":175.83,"b":178.7,"text":"Keep practising with Knowzone, and you'll master government.","w":[175.88,176.12,176.68,176.88,177.48,177.58,177.8,178.2]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"Three arms of government","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"Who makes laws? Who carries them out? Who settles disputes?"},{"at":6.66,"kind":"text","text":"Three arms and how they work together"}]},{"at":11.8,"heading":"Legislature","items":[{"at":11.95,"kind":"term","text":"Legislature","detail":"Makes the laws"},{"at":17,"kind":"term","text":"National Assembly","detail":"1999 Constitution: Senate + House of Representatives"},{"at":26.54,"kind":"text","text":"State level: State Houses of Assembly"}]},{"at":29.76,"heading":"Executive & Judiciary","items":[{"at":29.91,"kind":"term","text":"Executive","detail":"Carries out laws; runs day-to-day government"},{"at":37.89,"kind":"text","text":"States: Governor + commissioners + civil service"},{"at":42.26,"kind":"term","text":"Judiciary","detail":"Interprets laws, settles disputes; Supreme Court + Chief Justice"}]},{"at":50.96,"heading":"Extra duties","items":[{"at":51.11,"kind":"text","text":"Legislature: budget, appointments, oversight"},{"at":57.76,"kind":"text","text":"Executive: budget, signs bills, foreign relations, law/order"},{"at":63.56,"kind":"term","text":"Judicial review","detail":"Courts can declare an action unconstitutional"}]},{"at":70.99,"heading":"Separation & checks","items":[{"at":71.14,"kind":"term","text":"Separation of powers","detail":"Functions in different hands; Baron de Montesquieu"},{"at":78.4,"kind":"term","text":"Checks and balances","detail":"Each arm limits the others; e.g. President vetoes a bill"},{"at":84.44,"kind":"text","text":"Override veto (2/3), impeach; courts strike down laws"},{"at":90.68,"kind":"text","text":"Ghana/West Africa: Parliament as legislature"}]},{"at":96.83,"heading":"Worked example: school safety","items":[{"at":96.98,"kind":"text","text":"Legislature debates and passes bill"},{"at":102.17,"kind":"text","text":"President signs; ministry carries it out"},{"at":106.96,"kind":"text","text":"Judiciary interprets; decides if constitutional"}]},{"at":112.33,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":112.33,"kind":"question","text":"Which arm of government interprets the law?"}]},{"at":119.23,"heading":"Answer","items":[{"at":119.38,"kind":"text","text":"Judiciary interprets the law"},{"at":119.38,"kind":"text","text":"Executive enforces; courts settle disputes"}]},{"at":126.56,"heading":"Common slips","items":[{"at":126.71,"kind":"wrong","text":"Executive makes laws"},{"at":126.71,"kind":"right","text":"Legislature makes laws"},{"at":133.33,"kind":"wrong","text":"Separation of powers = each arm limits others"},{"at":133.33,"kind":"right","text":"Separation: functions in different hands"},{"at":141.4,"kind":"wrong","text":"Judiciary enforces laws"}]},{"at":148.57,"heading":"Recap","items":[{"at":148.72,"kind":"text","text":"Legislature makes; executive implements; courts interpret"},{"at":155.8,"kind":"term","text":"Nigeria's three arms","detail":"National Assembly bicameral; President + ministers; Supreme Court"},{"at":163.77,"kind":"text","text":"Separation: own roles; checks: arms limit each other"},{"at":170.22,"kind":"tip","text":"JAMB/WAEC: match each function to the correct arm"},{"at":175.73,"kind":"text","text":"Keep practising with Knowzone"}]}],"check":{"at":118.88,"seconds":10,"question":"Which arm of government interprets the law?","answer":"The judiciary interprets the law."},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}